From the image gradient, we can extract a number of interesting quantities: 

$$\begin{aligned}
    e(x,y) &= \lVert \nabla \ell(x,y) \rVert   & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{edge strength}\\
    \theta(x,y) &= \angle \nabla \ell =  \arctan \left( \frac{\partial \ell / \partial y}{\partial \ell / \partial x} \right) & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{edge orientation}
\end{aligned}$$ 

The edge strength is the gradient magnitude, and the edge orientation is perpendicular to the gradient direction.

In a 3D vertical edge, using the projection equations, the derivative of $Y$ along the edge will be 

$$\begin{aligned}
\partial Y / \partial y &= 1/ \cos(\theta)\end{aligned}
$$

Priors $p(f)$ can be used for inferring the most probable hypothesis. When a prior is used in conjunction with a likelihood function, we arrive at maximum a posteriori learning (MAP learning), which infers the most probable hypothesis given the training data:

$$\begin{aligned}
    &\mathop{\mathrm{arg\,max}}_f p\big(f \bigm | \{\mathbf{x}^{(i)}, \mathbf{y}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N\big) \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \text{MAP learning}\\
    & = \mathop{\mathrm{arg\,max}}_f p\big(\{\mathbf{y}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N \bigm | \{\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N, f\big)p\big(f\big) \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \text{by Bayes' rule}
\end{aligned}$$

Our objective is that predictions should be near ground truth targets in a least-squares sense, that is, $(\hat{y}^{(i)} - y^{(i)})^2$ should be small for all training examples $\{x^{(i)}, y^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N$. We call this objective the $L_2$ loss:

$$\begin{aligned}
    J(\theta) &= \sum_i \mathcal{L}(\hat{y}^{(i)}, y^{(i)})\\
    &\quad \mathcal{L}(\hat{y}, y) = (\hat{y} - y)^2 \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad L_2 \text{ loss}
\end{aligned}$$

We will use $J(\theta)$ to denote the total objective, over all training datapoints, as a function of the parameters; we will use $\mathcal{L}$ to denote the loss per datapoint. That is, $J(\theta) = \sum_{i=1}^N \mathcal{L}(f_{\theta}(x^{(i)}), y^{(i)})$.

Because minimizing misclassifications is NP-hard, people commonly use the cross-entropy loss, which is continuous and differentiable (making it easier to optimize):
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathcal{L}(\hat{\mathbf{y}},\mathbf{y}) = H(\mathbf{y}, \hat{\mathbf{y}}) = - \sum_{k=1}^K y_k \log \hat{y}_k \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \text{cross-entropy loss}
\end{aligned}$$

The way to think about this is $\hat{y}_k$ should represent the probability we think the image is an image of class $k$. Under that interpretation, minimizing cross-entropy maximizes the log likelihood of the ground truth observation $\mathbf{y}$ under our model's prediction $\hat{\mathbf{y}}$.

We can describe the photoreceptor responses using matrix algebra. If the matrix $\mathbf{C}_{\mbox{eye}}$ consists of the spectral sensitivities of the L, M, and S cones in three rows, and the vector $\mathbf{t}$ is a column vector of the spectrum of light incident on the eye, then the L, M, and S cone responses will be the product:
$$\begin{bmatrix}
    L  \\
    M  \\
    S \\
    \end{bmatrix}
= \mathbf{C}_{\mbox{eye}} \, \mathbf{t}
$$

Empirical Risk Minimization (ERM) is a formalization of learning from examples. This framework applies specifically to the supervised setting where we are learning a function that predicts $\mathbf{y}$ from $\mathbf{x}$ given many training examples $\{\mathbf{x}^{(i)},\mathbf{y}^{(i)}\}^N_{i=1}$. The idea is to minimize the average error (i.e., risk) we incur over all the training data (i.e., empirical distribution). The ERM problem is stated as follows:

$$
\begin{aligned}
    \mathop{\mathrm{arg\,min}}_{f \in \mathcal{F}} \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \mathcal{L}(f(\mathbf{x}^{(i)}),\mathbf{y}^{(i)}) \quad\triangleleft\quad \text{ERM}
\end{aligned}
$$

Here, $\mathcal{F}$ is the hypothesis space, $\mathcal{L}$ is the loss function, and $\{\mathbf{x}^{(i)}, \mathbf{y}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N$ is the training data (example {`input`, `output`} pairs), and $f$ is the learned function.

Depending on the loss function, there is often an interpretation of ERM as doing maximum likelihood probabilistic inference. In this interpretation, we are trying to infer the hypothesis $f$ that assigns the highest probability to the data. For a model that predicts $\mathbf{y}$ given $\mathbf{x}$, the max likelihood $f$ is:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathop{\mathrm{arg\,max}}_f p\big(\{\mathbf{y}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N \bigm | \{\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N, f\big) \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad\text{Max likelihood learning}
\end{aligned}
$$

The term $p\big(\{\mathbf{y}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N \bigm | \{\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N, f\big)$ is called the likelihood of the $\mathbf{y}$ values given the model $f$ and the observed $\mathbf{x}$ values, and maximizing this quantity is called maximum likelihood learning.

If the distance from the sensing plane to the pinhole is $f$, then similar triangles give us the following relations:

$$\begin{align}
x &= f \frac{X}{Z}
\end{align}$$

$$\begin{align}
y &= f \frac{Y}{Z} 
\end{align}$$

Thales of Miletus introduced the notion of similar triangles in 624 B.C., reportedly using them to measure the height of Egyptian pyramids and the distance to boats at sea.

Linear layers are the workhorses of deep nets. Almost all parameters of the network are contained in these layers; these parameters are called the weights and biases. They are defined by the operation: 

$$
\begin{aligned}
    \textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}} = \textcolor[rgb]{0.2, 0.6, 1.0}{\mathbf{W}} \textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}} + \textcolor[rgb]{0.2, 0.6, 1.0}{\mathbf{b}} & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad\texttt{linear}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
\begin{aligned}
    \textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{out}}}[i] &= 
        \begin{cases}
            1, &\text{if} \quad \textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i] > 0\\
            0,              & \text{otherwise}
        \end{cases} & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{threshold}
\end{aligned}
$$

$$
    \textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{out}}}[i] = \frac{1}{1 + e^{-\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i]}} \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{sigmoid}
$$

Euclid listed the following seven axioms of light:

1. Let it be assumed that lines draw directly from the eye pass through a space of great extent;
2. and that the form of the space included within our vision is a cone, with its apex in the eye and its base at the limits of our vision;
3. and that those things upon which vision falls are seen, and that those things upon which vision does not fall are not seen;
4. and that those things seen within a larger angle appear larger, and that those seen within a smaller angle appear smaller, and those seen within equal angles appear to be of the same size;
5. and that things seen within the higher visual range appear higher, while those within the lower range appear lower;
6. and, similarly, that those seen within the visual range on the right appear on the right, while those within that on the left appear on the left;
7. but that things seen within several angles appear to be more clear.

The trick of backpropagation is the reuse of computation. Consider a computation graph that is a chain of functions $f_L \circ f_{L-1} \circ \cdots f_2 \circ f_1$, with each function $f_l$ parameterized by $\theta_l$. To optimize these parameters with respect to a loss function $\mathcal{L}$ (outputting loss $J$), we need to compute gradients $\frac{\partial J}{\partial \theta_1}, \frac{\partial J}{\partial \theta_2}$, etc., via the chain rule.

(see figure: Basic sequential computation graph.)

(see figure: Computation graph as a tree.)

When writing out the chain rule for different parameters, we notice that most terms are shared:

$$\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial J}{\partial \theta_1}
&=
\colorbox{gray!20}{% 
  $\displaystyle\mathstrut \frac{\partial J}{\partial \mathbf{x}_L} \frac{\partial \mathbf{x}_L}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{L-1}} \cdots \frac{\partial \mathbf{x}_3}{\partial \mathbf{x}_2} $
}\, 
\frac{\partial \mathbf{x}_2}{\partial \mathbf{x}_1} 
\frac{\partial \mathbf{x}_1}{\partial \theta_1}
\\
\frac{\partial J}{\partial \theta_2}
&=
\colorbox{gray!20}{% 
  $\displaystyle\mathstrut \frac{\partial J}{\partial \mathbf{x}_L} \frac{\partial \mathbf{x}_L}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{L-1}} \cdots \frac{\partial \mathbf{x}_3}{\partial \mathbf{x}_2} $
}\, 
\frac{\partial \mathbf{x}_2}{\partial \theta_2}
\end{aligned}$$

Rather than evaluating these separately, we calculate each shared term once and reuse them. This strategy is an application of dynamic programming.

While backpropagation is often explained for a single datapoint, the total cost function is typically the average of the losses over all datapoints in a training set, $\{\mathbf{x}^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^N$. Because the gradient of a sum is the sum of the gradients, we can compute the gradient for the whole dataset using the identity:

$$\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial \frac{1}{N}\sum_{i=1}^N J_i(\theta)}{\partial \theta} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \frac{\partial J_i(\theta)}{\partial \theta}
\end{aligned}$$

In batch mode, backpropagation is run over each datapoint in the batch (often in parallel), and the results are averaged to perform a gradient update for algorithms like stochastic gradient descent.

The unit norm vector perpendicular to an edge is:

$$\begin{aligned}
{\bf n} = \frac{\nabla \ell}{\lVert \nabla \ell \rVert}
\end{aligned}$$

Upsampling consists in the sequence of expansion followed by interpolation:

$$\begin{aligned}
    z \left[n,m\right] &= \ell_{\texttt{in}}\left[n,m\right] \uparrow k 
  & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{expansion}\\
    \ell_{\texttt{out}}[n,m] &= z \left[n,m\right] \circ h_k \left[n,m\right]  & \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{conv}
\end{aligned}$$

Upsampling by a factor of 2 is done by inserting one 0 between each two samples (first for the horizontal dimension and then for the vertical one), and then interpolating between samples applying the binomial filter $\left[1/2,1,1/2\right] \circ \left[1/2,1,1/2\right]^\mathsf{T}$.

@fig-upsamplingazebra shows the upsampling process for a color image. Each color channel is upsampled by a factor of $k=2$ independently. The top row shows the input image, the expanded image by inserting zeros between samples, the binomial kernel and the resulting interpolated image. The bottom row shows the magnitude of DFTs of each corresponding image. Note that the binomial interpolation filter does not completely suppress the copies of the original DFT that appear when inserting new samples. Remember that the DFTs have the same number of samples as the corresponding input image. The DFT of the kernel is shown by zero padding the convolutional kernel to match the image size. Despite that this interpolation does correspond to an ideal interpolation filter (a sinc) the interpolated image looks reasonably good.

(see figure: Upsampling by a factor of $k=2$ using a bilinear interpolation filter.)

The spatial derivative can be approximated using $d_1 = [1, 0, -1]/2$. In one dimension, convolving a signal $\ell [n]$ with $d_1 [n]$ results in:
$$
\ell \circ d_1 = \frac{\ell [n+1] - \ell [n-1]}{2}
$$
This addresses the alignment issues of simpler filters. The output shows the highest magnitude at the midpoint where there is variation in the input signal, ensuring the derivative is centered correctly relative to the input.

![ (a) Input signal, $\ell [n]$. (b) Convolutional kernel $d_0 [n]$, defined as $d_0 [0]=1$ and $d_0 [1]=-1$ and zero everywhere else. (c) Output of the convolution between $\ell [n]$ and $d_0 [n]$. (d) Kernel $d_1 [n]$, defined as $d_1 [-1]=1$ and $d_1 [1]=-1$ and zero everywhere else. (e) Output of the convolution between $\ell [n]$ and $d_1 [n]$.](figures/derivatives/discretederivative.png){width=70% #fig-discretederivative}

In the context of ResNets, skip connections are called residual connections. In a residual block, this connection is added to the output of a block of layers $F$:
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}= F(\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}) + \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}\quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{residual block}
\end{aligned}$$
You can think of $F$ as a residual that additively perturbs $\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}$ to transform it into an improved $\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}$. If $\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}$ does not have the same dimensionality as $\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}$, a linear mapping $\mathbf{W}$ can be added to convert the dimensions: $\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}= F(\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}) + \mathbf{W}\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}$.

The $\texttt{leaky-relu}$ activation function is defined as:
$$\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{out}}}[i] = 
        \begin{cases}
            \max(\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i],0), &\text{if} \quad \textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i] \geq 0\\
            \textcolor[rgb]{0.2, 0.6, 1.0}{\alpha}\min(\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i],0),              & \text{otherwise}
        \end{cases}$$

Batchnorm standardizes each neural activation with respect to its mean and variance over a batch of datapoints. Mathematically:
$$\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{out}}}[i] = 
    \textcolor[rgb]{0.2, 0.6, 1.0}{\gamma}
    \frac{\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i] - \mathbb{E}[\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i]]}
         {\sqrt{\texttt{Var}[\textcolor[rgb]{1.0, 0.4, 0.38}{x_{\texttt{in}}}[i]]}} 
    + \textcolor[rgb]{0.2, 0.6, 1.0}{\beta}$$
where $\textcolor[rgb]{0.2, 0.6, 1.0}{\gamma}$ and $\textcolor[rgb]{0.2, 0.6, 1.0}{\beta}$ are learned parameters that maintain expressivity, allowing the layer to output values with non-zero mean and non-unit variance. Most commonly, batchnorm is applied using training batch statistics which change batch to batch. At test time, aggregate statistics from the training data are used, though test batch statistics can be used to achieve invariance to changes in data statistics.

A token-wise MLP applies the same MLP $F_{\theta}$ to each token in a sequence. Assuming no biases for simplicity, the linear layers in $F_{\theta}$ take the form:
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{t}_{\texttt{out}} &= \mathbf{W}\mathbf{t}_{\texttt{in}}
\end{aligned}$$
When applied to each token in a sequence, this becomes:
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}}&= \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}\mathbf{W}^{\mathsf{T}}
\end{aligned}$$
These linear layers can be written as convolutions with kernel size 1. Since the pointwise nonlinearities in the MLP are applied neuronwise, they function identically to the pointwise nonlinearities in CNNs. Therefore, a token-wise MLP is equivalent to a CNN consisting of a series of convolutions interleaved with neuronwise nonlinearities.

In the continuous domain, the relationship between the Fourier transform of a function and the Fourier transform of its derivative is given by:
$$
\frac{\partial \ell (x)}{\partial x}  
\xrightarrow{\mathscr{F}} 
j w \mathscr{L} (w)
$$

When working with two-dimensional (2D) images, partial image derivatives can be approximated by computing derivatives along the $n$ and $m$ components using kernels such as:
$$
\begin{bmatrix}
  1 \\
  -1
\end{bmatrix}
~~~~~~~
\begin{bmatrix}
  1 & -1
\end{bmatrix}
$$
A known issue with this discretization is that the outputs are spatially misaligned; a filter of length 2 shifts the output image by half a pixel. This spatial misalignment can be addressed by using a rotated reference frame, as seen in the Roberts cross operator.

The Roberts cross operator, introduced in 1963, is a method for approximating image derivatives that avoids the half-pixel spatial misalignment caused by simple length-2 filters. It achieves this by using a rotated reference frame with the following kernels:
$$
\begin{bmatrix}
  1 & ~0\\
  0 & -1
\end{bmatrix}
~~~~~~~
\begin{bmatrix}
  ~0 & 1 \\
  -1 & 0
\end{bmatrix}
$$

Pooling layers are downsampling layers that summarize the information in a patch using some aggregate statistic, such as the patch's mean value, called mean pooling, or its max value, called max pooling, defined as follows:

$$\begin{aligned}    
x_{\texttt{out}}[i]= \max_{i \in \mathcal{N}(i)} x_{\texttt{in}}[i]& \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{max pooling}\\
    x_{\texttt{out}}[i]= \frac{1}{|\mathcal{N}|} \sum_{i \in \mathcal{N}(i)} x_{\texttt{in}}[i]& \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad \texttt{mean pooling}
\end{aligned}$$

The $\mathcal{N}(i)$ indicates the set of indices in the same patch as index $i$.

The indicator function, denoted by $\mathbb{1}$, is defined as: 
$$\begin{equation*}
    \mathbb{1}(x) = \begin{cases}
                1 & \text{if $x$ is true} \\
                0 & \text{otherwise}
             \end{cases}
\end{equation*}$$

Linear combinations only let us linearly mix and recombine tokens, and stacking linear functions can only result in another linear function. To get around this limitation, we introduce tokenwise operators; these are functions that apply a nonlinear transformation to each token individually, independently from all other tokens. Given a nonlinear function $F_{	heta}: \mathbb{R}^N \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^N$, a tokenwise nonlinearity layer, taking input $\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}$, can be expressed as:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}}= 
        \begin{bmatrix}
        F_{\theta}(\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}[0,:]) \\
        \vdots \\
        F_{\theta}(\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}[N-1,:]) \\
        \end{bmatrix}\quad\quad \triangleleft \quad\text{per-token nonlinearity}
\end{aligned}$$

Notice that this operation is a generalization of the pointwise nonlinearity in regular neural nets; a relu layer is the special case where $F_{\theta} = \texttt{relu}$ and the layer operates over a set of neuron inputs (scalars) rather than token inputs (vectors):

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}= 
        \begin{bmatrix}
        \texttt{relu}(x_{\texttt{in}}[0]) \\
        \vdots \\
        \texttt{relu}(x_{\texttt{in}}[N-1]) \\
        \end{bmatrix}\quad\quad \triangleleft \quad\text{per-neuron nonlinearity (\texttt{relu})}
\end{aligned}$$

The $F_{\theta}$ may be any nonlinear function, but one popular choice is for $F_{\theta}$ to be a multilayer perceptron (MLP). In this case, $F_{\theta}$ has learnable parameters $\theta$, which are the weights and biases of the MLP. This reveals an important difference between pointwise operations in regular neural nets and in token nets: whereas relus have no learnable parameters, $F_{\theta}$ typically does, making the pointwise operations expressive and parameter-rich.

Transformers use a particular kind of attention based on the idea of queries, keys, and values. In query-key-value attention, each token is associated with a query vector, a key vector, and a value vector. The idea comes from databases, where a database cell holds a value, which is retrieved when a query matches the cell's key. Tokens are like database cells and attention is like retrieving information from the database of tokens.

We define these vectors as linear transformations of the token's code vector $\mathbf{t}$, projecting to query/key/value vectors of length $m$:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{q} &= \mathbf{W}_q \mathbf{t} \quad\quad \triangleleft \text{ query}\\
    \mathbf{k} &= \mathbf{W}_k \mathbf{t} \quad\quad \triangleleft \text{ key}\\
    \mathbf{v} &= \mathbf{W}_v \mathbf{t} \quad\quad \triangleleft \text{ value}
\end{aligned}$$

A query vector $\mathbf{q}$ is matched against the keys of other tokens; the similarity between the query and the key determines the amount of attention weight. The most common measure of similarity is the dot product $\mathbf{q}^\mathsf{T}\mathbf{k}$. Querying each token in $\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}$ gives a vector of similarities:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{s} = [s_1, \ldots, s_N]^\mathsf{T}&= [\mathbf{q}^\mathsf{T}\mathbf{k}_1, \ldots, \mathbf{q}^\mathsf{T}\mathbf{k}_N]^\mathsf{T}
\end{aligned}$$

We then normalize the vector $\mathbf{s}$ using the softmax function to give us our attention weights $\mathbf{a} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times 1}$, and finally take a weighted sum over token value vectors to obtain $\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}}$:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{a} &= \texttt{softmax}(\mathbf{s})\\
    \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}}&= \begin{bmatrix}
        a_1\mathbf{v}_1^\mathsf{T}\\
        \vdots \\
        a_N\mathbf{v}_N^\mathsf{T}\\
    \end{bmatrix} 
\end{aligned}$$

[FIGURE:afb7764c48883324 | A legend showing the color coding for attention components: 'query' is represented by a yellow vertical rectangle, 'key' by a magenta vertical rectangle, and 'value' by a teal vertical rectangle.]

(see figure: Mechanics of an attention layer. Queries from the question match keys from the tokens representing the impala; value vectors of the impala tokens then contribute the most to the sum that yields...)

To compute the query, key, and value for a set of input tokens $\mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}$, we apply linear transformations to each token, resulting in matrices $\mathbf{Q}_{\texttt{in}}, \mathbf{K}_{\texttt{in}} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times m}$ and $\mathbf{V}_{\texttt{in}} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times d}$. The query and key vectors must have the same dimensionality $m$ for the dot product, while value vectors match the token code dimensionality $d$.

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{Q}_{\texttt{in}} &= \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}\mathbf{W}_q^\mathsf{T} \quad\quad \triangleleft \text{ query matrix} \\
    \mathbf{K}_{\texttt{in}} &= \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}\mathbf{W}_k^\mathsf{T} \quad\quad \triangleleft \text{ key matrix}\\
    \mathbf{V}_{\texttt{in}} &= \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{in}}\mathbf{W}_v^\mathsf{T} \quad\quad \triangleleft \text{ value matrix}
\end{aligned}$$

The attention equation is then defined as:

$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{A} &= \texttt{softmax}\Big(\frac{\mathbf{Q}_{\texttt{in}}\mathbf{K}_{\texttt{in}}^\mathsf{T}}{\sqrt{m}}\Big) \quad\quad \triangleleft \text{ attention matrix}\\
    \mathbf{T}_{\texttt{out}} &= \mathbf{A}\mathbf{V}_{\texttt{in}}
\end{aligned}$$

where the softmax is taken within each row. This process allows for the simultaneous matching of all queries against all keys.

(see figure: Self-attention layer expanded. The nodes with the dashed outline correspond to each other; they represent one query being matched against one key to result in a scalar similarity value, in the gray...)

The prior probability of any configuration of pixel disparity states is described by setting the compatibility matrices, $\mathbf{\phi}[s_i, s_{i+1}]$ of the Markov chain. For this example, we use a compatibility matrix referred to as the Potts Model: 

$$\mathbf{\phi}[s_i, s_{i+1}] = 
    \begin{cases}
      1, & \text{if}\ s_i = s_{i+1} \\
      \delta, & \text{otherwise}
    \end{cases}$$

A recurrent layer is defined by the following equations: 
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{h}_t &= f(\mathbf{h}_{t-1}, \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[t]) &\triangleleft \quad\quad \text{update state based on input and previous state}\\
    \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}[t]&= g(\mathbf{h}_t) &\triangleleft \quad\quad \text{produce output based on state}
\end{aligned}$$ 
This is a layer that includes a state variable, $\mathbf{h}$. The operation of the layer depends on its state. The layer also updates its state every time it is called; therefore, the state is a kind of memory.

(see figure: Backpropagation through time in an RNN with a loss at each timestep.)

Running an RNN for many timesteps results in very large computation graphs. The gradient computation involves a series of matrix multiplies that is $\mathcal{O}(T)$ in length. For example, computing the gradient $\frac{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}[t]}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[0]}$ involves the following product: 

$$\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}[t]}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[0]} = \frac{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{out}}[t]}{\partial \mathbf{h}_T} \frac{\partial \mathbf{h}_T}{\partial \mathbf{h}_{T-1}} \cdots \frac{\partial \mathbf{h}_1}{\partial \mathbf{h}_0} \frac{\partial \mathbf{h}_0}{\partial \mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[0]}
\end{aligned}$$ 

In an RNN with ReLU nonlinearities, each term $\frac{\partial \mathbf{h}_t}{\partial \mathbf{h}_{t-1}}$ equals $\mathbf{R}\mathbf{W}$, where $\mathbf{R}$ is a diagonal matrix with zeros or ones on the $i$-th diagonal element. In a worst-case scenario where all ReLUs are active, we have a product of $T$ matrices $\mathbf{W}$, effectively $\mathbf{W}^T$. If values in $\mathbf{W}$ are large, the gradient will explode; if they are small, the gradient will vanish, making the system numerically unstable.

One component of an LSTM picks the indices of the cell state to *delete*: 
$$\begin{aligned}
    \mathbf{f}_t &= \sigma(\mathbf{W}_f\mathbf{h}_{t-1} + \mathbf{U}_f\mathbf{x}_{\texttt{in}}[t] + \mathbf{b}_f) &\triangleleft \quad\quad\text{decide which indices to forget}
\end{aligned}$$ 
where $\mathbf{f}_t$ is a vector of length equal to the length of the cell state $\mathbf{c}_t$, and $\sigma$ is the sigmoid function that outputs values in the range $[0,1]$. The idea is that most values in $\mathbf{f}_t$ will be near either $1$ (remember) or 0 (forget). Later $\mathbf{f}_t$ will be pointwise multiplied by the cell state to forget the values where $\mathbf{f}_t$ is zero.

To handle cases where $y(\mathbf{x}) \neq y(T(\mathbf{x},\theta))$, we use a more advanced kind of data augmentation where we transform the $\mathbf{y}$ values at the same time as the $\mathbf{x}$ values, using transforms $T_{\mathcal{X}}$ and $T_{\mathcal{Y}}$. This results in an augmented dataset of the form $\{T_{\mathcal{X}}(\mathbf{x}^{(i)};\theta^{(j)}), T_{\mathcal{Y}}(\mathbf{y}^{(i)};\theta^{(j)})\}_{j=1}^M$. This requires that $T_{\mathcal{X}}$ and $T_{\mathcal{Y}}$ express an equivariance between the two domains:
$$\begin{aligned}
     T_{\mathcal{Y}}(y(x),\theta_{\mathcal{Y}}) = y(T_{\mathcal{X}}(x,\theta_{\mathcal{X}})) \quad \forall \theta \quad\quad \triangleleft \quad\text{$y$ equivariant with respect to $T_\mathcal{X}, T_\mathcal{Y}$}
\end{aligned}$$
This is standard when $\mathbf{y}$ has spatial or temporal structure aligned with $\mathbf{x}$, such as in semantic segmentation. In that case, a random crop applied to the input image $\mathbf{x}$ must also be applied to the label map $\mathbf{y}$.

In perspective projection, 3D lines project into 2D lines in the camera plane. We can parameterize a 3D line with a point, $\mathbf{P}_0=[X_0, Y_0, Z_0]^\mathsf{T}$, and a direction vector, $\mathbf{D} = [D_X, D_Y, D_Z]^\mathsf{T}$, as:

$$\mathbf{P}_t = 
    \begin{bmatrix}
    X_0 + t D_x\\
    Y_0 + t D_y\\
    Z_0 + t D_z
    \end{bmatrix}
$$

The 3D point $\mathbf{P}_t$ moves along a line when the independent variable $t$ goes from $-\infty$ to $\infty$. A camera located in the world coordinates origin will observe a 2D line resulting from projecting the 3D line into the camera plane.

(see figure: Projection of a 3D line onto the camera plane. The 3D line and the camera center form a plane (the camera center is a the world-coordinates origin). The intersection of this plane with the camera...)

We can get the equation of the 2D line that appears in the image by using the perspective projection equations, resulting in:
$$\mathbf{p}_t = 
    \begin{bmatrix}
    x_t\\
    y_t
    \end{bmatrix}
    =
    \begin{bmatrix}
    f \dfrac{ X_0 + t D_X}{Z_0 + t D_Z}\\[12pt]
    f \dfrac{ Y_0 + t D_Y}{Z_0 + t D_Z}
    \end{bmatrix}
$$

The vanishing point, in homogeneous coordinates, is the result of projecting $\mathbf{P}_\infty$ using the camera model $\mathbf{M}$:

$$\mathbf{v} = 
    \mathbf{M} 
    \mathbf{P}_\infty 
    = 
    \mathbf{K} 
    \begin{bmatrix}
    \mathbf{R} & \mathbf{T} 
    \end{bmatrix}
    \begin{bmatrix}
    \mathbf{D}\\
    0
    \end{bmatrix}
    = 
    \mathbf{K} \mathbf{R} \mathbf{D}
$$

This shows that the vanishing point coordinates do not depend on camera translation. Only a camera rotation will change the location of the vanishing points in an image. If a scene is captured by two identical cameras translated from each other, their corresponding images will have the same vanishing points.

Consider rotation along the camera optical axis, where $W_X=W_Y=0$. In this case the motion field is:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= 
\begin{bmatrix}
 y \\
-x  
\end{bmatrix}
W_Z 
\end{aligned}$$

The 2D motion field at each location $(x,y)$ will point in the orthogonal direction to the vector that connects that point with the origin (@fig-motion_wz). The motion field does not depend on the focal length, $f$.

(see figure: Motion field for a rotating camera around the optical axis, $W_X=W_Y=0$.)

If $W_Z=0$, rotation along $W_X$ or $W_Y$ produces similar 2D motion fields. Considering $W_X=0$, the motion field is:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= 
-\frac{1}{f}
\begin{bmatrix}
f^2+x^2 \\
xy  
\end{bmatrix}
W_Y 
\end{aligned}$$

In this case, the focal length $f$ has a strong effect on the appearance of the motion field. For very large $f$, the 2D motion field can be approximated by $\dot{x} \approx f W_Y$ and $\dot{y} \approx 0$. This resulting field is approximately constant across the entire image and resembles lateral translation motion. For very small $f$, the motion field is similar to one produced by a homography and differs significantly from lateral camera motion. The flows shown in @fig-motion_wy_f03_f1_f3 correspond to $f=1/3$, $f=1$, and $f=3$.

(see figure: Motion flows corresponding to a rotation around the Y-axis. (left) f = 1/3. (middle) f = 1. (right) f = 3.)

Camera rotation around the $Y$-axis does not provide information about scene structure, but it does inform about camera parameters. The magnitude of $W_Y$ affects the scaling of the motion vectors but does not change their orientation.

Consider a static camera observing a static scene where the focal length $f$ changes over time. Despite the lack of scene motion, the changing focal length produces motion in the image plane. If the focal length increases, it results in a zoom effect. Starting from the perspective projection equation:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
x \\
y
\end{bmatrix}
= \frac{f}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
X \\
Y
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

Computing the temporal derivative where only $f$ varies gives:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= \frac{\dot{f}}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
X \\
Y
\end{bmatrix}
= 
\frac{\dot{f}}{f}
\begin{bmatrix}
x \\
y
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

Changing the focal length results only in a scaling of the projected image and does not create parallax. Because the sensor has a finite size, this manifests as a zoom and a crop. The motion field is independent of the 3D scene structure; therefore, images taken from the same viewpoint with different focal lengths do not provide depth information. This demonstrates a 2D motion field occurring without physical motion in the scene.

When a camera moves in a straight line with a velocity $\dot{\mathbf{P}} = -\mathbf{V}$, where $\mathbf{V} = (V_X, V_Y, V_Z)^\mathsf{T}$, the observed motion in the image plane is equivalent to all scene points moving relative to the camera with that same velocity. The 2D motion field is given by:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= \frac{1}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
-f & 0 & x \\
0 & -f & y  
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
V_X\\
V_Y \\
V_Z 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

This relationship can be rearranged to make the contribution of the camera coordinates more explicit:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= -\frac{f}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
V_X\\
V_Y 
\end{bmatrix}
+
\frac{V_Z}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
x\\
y 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

This provides a generic expression for the observed motion in the camera plane produced by a moving camera undergoing translation without rotation.

Consider a camera translating laterally, where the forward velocity is zero ($V_Z=0$). This occurs, for example, when looking through a side window of a car at a passing scene.

(see figure: Lateral camera motion parallel to the camera plane.)

Under lateral camera motion, the relationship between the velocity of a 3D point and the apparent velocity of its projection in the image plane is:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= 
-\frac{f}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
 V_X \\
V_Y  
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

The motion field depends on the depth $Z$ at each location. Objects close to the camera appear to move faster than objects farther away, while very distant objects appear stationary. This parallax effect is the same principle used in stereo vision to recover depth. The 2D motion in the image plane is in the opposite direction to the camera motion.

(see figure: Sketch of the motion field under lateral camera motion. Objects close to the camera will appear to be moving faster than objects farther away. Objects that are very far (like the cloud) will appear...)

For a camera moving forward, the velocity components $V_X$ and $V_Y$ are zero, and motion occurs only along the $Z$-axis ($V_Z \neq 0$).

(see figure: Camera moving forward, along the camera axis.)

In this case, the motion field is described by:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= 
\frac{V_Z}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
x \\
y  
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

For a camera moving in an arbitrary direction ($V_X \neq 0, V_Y \neq 0, V_Z \neq 0$), the motion field can be expressed as:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
=
\frac{V_Z}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
x - x_{\infty}\\
y - y_{\infty} 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

The observed motion is zero at the focus of expansion, $(x_{\infty}, y_{\infty})$. Points near this center (the point of impact) appear stationary, while points in the periphery move faster and away from the center, causing the image to expand over time.

(see figure: Sketch of the motion field when the camera approaches a planar surface. The motion field indicates the rate of expansion of the image, and it is a function of the time to contact. In this example...)

For a camera undergoing both translation and rotation, the displacement of points relative to the camera coordinate system over a small time interval $\Delta t$ is $\mathbf{P}_{t+\Delta t} = - \mathbf{T}_{\Delta t}  + \mathbf{R}_{\Delta t} \mathbf{P}_t$. The velocity of a 3D point is $\dot{\mathbf{P}} = - \mathbf{V} - \mathbf{W} \times \mathbf{P}_t$, where $\mathbf{V}$ is linear velocity and $\mathbf{W}$ is angular velocity $(W_X, W_Y, W_Z)$.

(see figure: Rotation expressed by Euler angles (yaw, pitch, roll).)

Using small-angle approximations for the rotation matrix and the relationship $x=f X/Z, y=f Y/Z$, the 2D motion field is:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= \frac{1}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
-f & 0 & x \\
0 & -f & y  
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
V_X\\
V_Y \\
V_Z 
\end{bmatrix}
+
\frac{1}{f}
\begin{bmatrix}
xy & -f^2-x^2) & f y \\
f^2+y^2 & -xy & -f x  
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
W_X\\
W_Y \\
W_Z 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

This expression relates the 2D motion field to camera velocity, rotation, intrinsic parameters (focal length $f$), and camera coordinates. This formulation is valid for small displacements.

Rotation can be decomposed using Euler angles (yaw, pitch, roll) along the three axes.

(see figure: Rotation expressed by Euler angles (yaw, pitch, roll).)

Each angle measures the rotation along the camera-coordinate axes. The resulting rotation matrix $\mathbf{R}_{\Delta t}$ is the product of three individual rotation matrices:

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{R}_{\Delta t} = 
\begin{bmatrix}
\cos \theta_Z &  \sin \theta_Z & 0\\
-\sin \theta_Z &  \cos \theta_Z & 0\\
0 &  0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\cos \theta_Y &  0 & -\sin \theta_Y\\
0 &  1 & 0\\
\sin \theta_Y &  0 & \cos \theta_Y
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
1 &  0 & 0\\
0 & \cos \theta_X &  \sin \theta_X \\
0 & -\sin \theta_X &  \cos \theta_X 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

The signs of the angles reflect that a camera rotation is equivalent to the opposite rotation of a 3D point.

In the case of pure camera rotation ($V_X=V_Y=V_Z=0$), the motion field is given by:

$$\begin{aligned}
\begin{bmatrix}
\dot{x} \\
\dot{y}
\end{bmatrix}
= 
\frac{1}{f}
\begin{bmatrix}
xy & -f^2-x^2) & f y \\
f^2+y^2 & -xy & -f x  
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
W_X\\
W_Y \\
W_Z 
\end{bmatrix}
\end{aligned}$$

Notably, this 2D motion field does not depend on the 3D scene structure $Z$. It is solely a function of rotational velocity and camera parameters. Consequently, observing the motion field under pure rotation provides information about camera motion but reveals nothing about the scene's depth or structure.

[FIGURE:2c6cb957a032955d | A photograph of a camera on a tripod viewing a set of colored blocks on a surface. Two 3D coordinate systems are overlaid: a world coordinate system $(X, Y, Z)$ centered on the blocks, and a camera...]

The extrinsic camera parameters model the 3D rotation, which is mainly around the $X$-axis (ignoring the translation of the origin):
$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{R}
= 
\begin{bmatrix}
1 & 0 & 0\\
0 & \cos(\theta) & -\sin(\theta) \\ 
0 & \sin(\theta) & \cos(\theta) 
\end{bmatrix}.
\end{aligned}
$$

For multiclass prediction problems, it is often useful to look at the structure of mistakes made by the model. The confusion matrix summarizes both the overall performance of the classifier and also the percentage of times that two classes are confused by the classifier. The diagonal elements represent the top-1 performance for each class, while off-diagonal elements show the confusions.

(see figure: Confusion matrix for a three-way classification task. The numbers are percentages.)

The elements of the confusion matrix are calculated as:

$$C_{i,j} = 100 \frac{
    \sum_{t=1}^T \mathbb{1}( \hat{y}^{(t)} = j)  \mathbb{1} (y^{(t)} = i)
    }{
    \sum_{t=1}^T \mathbb{1}(y^{(t)} = i) 
    }$$

where $C_{i,j}$ measures the percentage of times that true class $i$ is classified as class $j$. In the example provided in (see figure: ...), 80 percent of cat images are correctly classified, while 5 percent are classified as cars and 15 percent as dogs.
